n forces that could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Tim Moore (R) is running."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's 14th District leans Republican by about eight points, and first-term Representative Tim Moore enters the general election with full incumbency advantages. He has raised roughly $1.5 million and holds about $1.6 million on hand, while Democratic challenger Lakesha Womack has raised about $25,000 and reports negative cash. Moore has spent the fall publicizing FEMA funding for Helene recovery, bipartisan disaster bills, and local appearances.\n\nRaters across the spectrum call the seat Safe or Likely Republican, and the only public poll, a March survey from a Republican-sponsored firm, showed Moore ahead 48-40 among likely voters. Prediction markets give Womack roughly a one-in-seven chance. The fundamentals point firmly toward Moore; the open question is the size of his winning margin rather than who wins the seat.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0644, "margin": -9.66}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0543, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0528, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0536, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0567, "margin": -10.5}]}